From: microvm <microvm@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] x86/CPU/AMD: don't read the S5 reset reason in a guest

print_s5_reset_status_mmio() maps the FCH power-management registers and
reads the previous reset reason. A guest has no FCH: the access is an MMIO
exit to the hypervisor, which can only answer with all-ones, and the
ioremap()/iounmap() pair costs a vmap purge on top. Skip it when running
under a hypervisor.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/amd.c	2026-10-30 13:06:41.000000000 +0100
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/amd.c	2026-11-04 08:38:25.849806394 +0100
@@ -1367,6 +1367,13 @@
 	if (!cpu_feature_enabled(X86_FEATURE_ZEN))
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * A guest has no FCH: the read traps to the hypervisor, which has no
+	 * reset reason to give.
+	 */
+	if (cpu_feature_enabled(X86_FEATURE_HYPERVISOR))
+		return 0;
+
 	addr = ioremap(FCH_PM_BASE + FCH_PM_S5_RESET_STATUS, sizeof(value));
 	if (!addr)
 		return 0;
